findings.body:
The condition in `setUserFeatureState` was flipped to `state !== "inherit"`, but the branch bodies still have their old shapes. Enabled and disabled now go through the call with no `assignedBy`, and inherit gets `assignedBy: "user-<id>"`, which the repository ignores for inherit.

The repository's enabled/disabled path reads `input.assignedBy` and passes it to `prismaClient.userFeatures.upsert`. With this change that value is `undefined`. Prisma will either reject the write or store a null `assignedBy`, depending on the schema. Either way, a user toggling a feature in `/settings/my-account/features` fails or writes incomplete audit data.

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="264-274"}

The new unit tests (`should set user feature state when scope allows` expects `assignedBy: "user-2"` for enabled, and the inherit test expects no `assignedBy`) would fail against this code. That means the test run is red, which the PR description does not mention.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="264-274"}
if (state === "inherit") {
  await this.featuresRepository.setUserFeatureState({ userId, featureId, state });
} else {
  const { assignedBy } = input;
  await this.featuresRepository.setUserFeatureState({
    userId,
    featureId,
    state,
    assignedBy: `user-${assignedBy}`,
  });
}

`listFeaturesForUser` now keeps features where `!state.globalEnabled`. The old filter kept globally enabled features, and that is the intended behavior. As written, the user's Features page lists globally disabled features and hides the ones that are actually available.

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="207-207"}

The doc comment directly above still says "globally enabled", so the code contradicts its own documentation. No test covers `listFeaturesForUser`, which is why this went unnoticed.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="207-207"}
    return featureIds.map((featureId) => resolvedStates[featureId]).filter((state) => state.globalEnabled);

In `setUserState`, the `featureOptInService.setUserFeatureState(...)` call lost its `await`. That breaks two things the PR relies on:

- The new `ErrorWithCode(BadRequest)` for out-of-scope features becomes an unhandled promise rejection. The client gets `{ success: true }` for a request that was rejected.
- Any repository error on the write, such as the Prisma failure from the finding above, is also unhandled, so the client is told the update succeeded.

The team and org mutations in the same router do await, so this is inconsistent with the rest of the file.

::diff{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/featureOptIn/_router.ts" lines="103-103"}

The PR description says scope errors are surfaced to the caller, and this breaks that promise for user-level writes.

